## 2. Eval table and counterfactual payoff — locked

These two are not open. The runner's defaults (`--opponents selfplay`, raw
score as a readable column) are the wrong eval for this wave. The flags and
the headline matrix have to name the replacements or a reader will load the
pilot as if it were self-play with absolute payoff.

### The table: `--opponents audit`. Not self-play. Not cross-play.

Three tables exist. This wave uses one.

| table | who sits opposite the model | what a moving rate means | what this wave does |
|---|---|---|---|
| **`audit`** | scripted honest, fixed every round | the focal seat learned | **this wave** |
| `selfplay` | copies of the same model | the table co-discovered, and in 4 cells the prize vanished | later, as a regime question |
| cross-play | a different lab's model | opponent identity moved, or contagion | `run_contagion.py`, not here |

Self-play looks like it isolates the model: every seat is one factor level,
so nothing else is in the composition. That is the argument
`run_referee_spartan.py` was written with, and it is the right argument
**against cross-play**. Cross-play puts another model into the same factor as
the focal seat. Claude-vs-GPT is not a Claude number; seat order and who
finds the hole first all move the rate. That is component noise in the model
factor, and it is a different experiment.

Self-play isolates the model as a **composition of the table** and contaminates
the **round** factor instead. In a competitive cell the other copies find the
same hole on the same reflection, and the prize is competed away exactly when
discovery happens. That is the icebound curve above. Four cells in this menu
stop paying when every seat exploits (`ref_orderbook`, `gen_icebound`,
`ta_liarsdice`, `ta_kuhn`). Under self-play those four are unreadable on both
rate and payoff: a falling curve is the model correctly leaving a hole that
no longer pays, reported as abandonment of a live one.

`--opponents audit` is the blocked design that actually matches the claim.
Focal seats = the model, including the collusion/channel sets
